  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nDespite our temperatures in the low 40s, it is a beautiful day \nacross the region with mostly sunny skies and little to no wind.\nOur quiet continues for several days as upper level ridging \nremains overhead. Southerly flow on the backside of the high \npressure will gradually increase temperatures for us starting \ntomorrow and lasting through the middle of the week. By \nWednesday, highs are expected to peak in the mid-upper 50s, \nwhich is nearly 15 degrees above normal for mid-November.\n\nOur next chance of precipitation will not come until late in the \nweek as a shortwave trough lifts northeast out of the Rockies on\nThursday. The latest NBM sprinkles 20 to 30 percent chances for\nrain as the low pressure system moves through, which looks \nreasonable based on somewhat lackluster forecast soundings. \nThere will likely be limited moisture available and relative \nweak forcing associated with the wave. Both WPC and the NBM have\nlittle to no QPF with this system for us. The bigger impact \nfrom this system will be the slight drop in temperatures. This \nis especially important as long range models continue to hint \ntowards a more organized low pressure system developing across \nthe central US some time next weekend. Within the warm sector of\nthe low, PWATs will likely climb to between 1 to 1.4\", which \nwould allow for better rainfall amounts. On the backside of the \nlow, temperatures may drop low enough for frozen ptype \nconcerns, however it does not appear that the push of cold air \nfrom the Thursday wave will be enough to make it a concern for \nour area. We'll have to watch how things develop, but the main \ntakeaway is that next weekend is the best period for active \nweather across the region in the next 7 days.",
